  "title": "Cheaper oil, a longer truce: who gains",
  "body": "Monday's markets moved on one input: **oil**. Brent fell almost 6% to about $92.6 after Trump said renewed strikes on Iran were unlikely, though it is still far above pre-war levels. Each of the three economies feels that differently.\n\n**US.** Stocks rose more than 2%, but the bond market didn't celebrate: the 10-year yield edged up to about 4.87%, and futures price roughly an 84% chance of a **rate hike** in December. Investors seem to think cheaper fuel won't quickly undo the inflation that has built up since February. This week's October CPI is the next test. (This is a reading of market pricing, not a forecast.)\n\n**India** imports most of its oil, so cheaper crude helps its trade balance and inflation. The Nifty closed at its highest since February, and foreign investors have put in over $4bn this month, partly money leaving the AI-heavy US tech trade, according to broker commentary.\n\n**Poland** gets some relief at the pump: the government's cap on petrol 95 fell to 6.04 zł from 6.12 zł. The złoty firmed even after S&P's negative outlook, although the rating agency's concern, debt rising amid political deadlock, is a slow-moving problem that oil prices don't fix.\n\n**Trade.** The US–China truce got two more months. Reports say the US formalised its suspension of port fees on China-linked ships, and Beijing extended its pause on the extra 24% tariff. That gives shippers breathing room into January, but it is a pause, not a settlement. The India–US deal is still unsigned ahead of the ASEAN summit in Manila on Nov 15.\n\nWatch whether Rubio's 'no progress' or Trump's 'effectively over' proves right. Oil, and with it all three stories, depends on it.",
